[38;2;124;124;124mWe have n distinct points A₁, A₂, ..., Aₙ. A 2-coloring is "good" if there exist[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthree points Aᵢ, Aⱼ, A₂ⱼ₋ᵢ with 1 ≤ i < 2j-i ≤ n that are all the same color.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote: Aᵢ, Aⱼ, A₂ⱼ₋ᵢ — these three indices form an arithmetic progression! [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBecause j-i = (2j-i)-j, so the indices i, j, 2j-i are in arithmetic progression [0m[38;2;124;124;124m(with common difference j-i). The condition 1 ≤ i < 2j-i ≤ n means we need a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mvalid 3-term arithmetic progression of indices, all the same color.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the question is: find the least n ≥ 3 such that every 2-coloring of {1, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2, ..., n} contains a monochromatic 3-term arithmetic progression.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is exactly the van der Waerden number W(3, 2) = 9.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mVan der Waerden number W(k, r) is the least n such that every r-coloring of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{1, ..., n} contains a monochromatic arithmetic progression of length k. W(3, 2)[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 9.[0m

